          "score": 0.482
        }
      ],
      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "The salt water behaves differently, because it's a mixture. Only the water molecules escape as gas. The ions from the salt stay behind. So as it boils, the water that's left gets saltier, and saltier water needs a higher temperature to boil. That's why its temperature keeps creeping up instead of staying flat. Something similar happens when an impure solid melts. The different particles disrupt its regular arrangement, so it starts melting at a lower temperature, and it melts gradually, over a range.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
