      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "So why does the pure water stop at a hundred degrees? While it heats, the energy makes the water molecules move faster, so the temperature rises. Once it boils, the energy goes into pulling molecules away from their neighbours and out into the gas. Every molecule is the same, so they can all escape at the same temperature, and the temperature stays flat until all the liquid is gone. Plot temperature against time and you get a heating curve. A pure substance gives a flat section, called a plateau, at its melting point and at its boiling point.",
